/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A linear block code is cyclic if every cyclic shift of a codeword is also a codeword.
   - Number the bits from the right.
   - c₀ is the rightmost bit of the word, cn−1 the leftmost; coefficients are 0 or 1 and addition is XOR.
   - Multiplying c(x) by x and reducing modulo xⁿ + 1 performs the cyclic shift: over GF(2), xⁿ − 1 and xⁿ + 1 are the same polynomial, so the top bit wraps round to the end.
   - Linearity means the XOR of two codewords is a codeword.
   - For the (7,4) code g(x) = x³ + x + 1.
   - Non-systematic encoding is one multiplication: c(x) = m(x)g(x), with m(x) = m₀ + m₁x + … + mk−1xk−1 carrying the k message bits.
   - Systematic form keeps the k message bits unchanged in the highest-order positions — the coefficients of xn−1 down to xn−k, sent first — and appends the n − k parity bits at the right end, in positions xn−k−1 down to x⁰.
   - m(x) is the message polynomial of degree at most k−1; b(x) holds the n−k parity bits; xn−km(x) means the message shifted left by n−k places.
   - Adding the remainder cancels it, so c(x) is a multiple of g(x) and hence a codeword.
*/
