/* book exam traps: Choosing a g(x) that does not divide xⁿ + 1. The code then fails the shift test and is not cyclic. | Attaching the parity bits on the left. Systematic means message in the high-order positions, parity at the right end. | Swapping g(x) and h(x): g(x) has degree n − k and generates the code; h(x) has degree k and checks it. | Borrowing in polynomial subtraction. Over GF(2), addition and subtraction are both XOR; no carries, no borrows. | Reading deg g as k. It is n − k, so k = n − deg g; a (7,4) code needs degree exactly 3. */
