/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - BCH stands for Bose–Chaudhuri–Hocquenghem.
   - The arithmetic runs in GF(2m), a field with 2m elements, formed by adjoining to GF(2) a root α of a primitive polynomial.
   - n is the block length in bits, m is the field exponent, t is the number of correctable errors, dmin is the minimum Hamming distance, g(x) is the generator polynomial, k is the number of message bits and R = k/n is the code rate.
   - Take m = 4, so n = 15.
   - Decoding works on polynomials: the syndromes give an error-locator polynomial, its roots give the error positions, and those bits are flipped.
   - Reed–Solomon codes use the same construction on symbols of GF(2m) instead of single bits.
   - A turbo code makes two encoders read the same block in different orders, then lets two decoders exchange soft opinions about every bit.
   - Encoding: block d enters a recursive systematic convolutional (RSC) encoder, which sends d out unchanged — systematic — and adds parity p1.
   - Recursive means the shift register has feedback, so its state depends on a running sum of past inputs.
   - Puncturing raises the rate by deleting parity bits.
*/
